Java 21 code examples

1. Eager initialization:
                          public class Singleton {
                              private static final Singleton INSTANCE = new Singleton();
                          
                              private Singleton() {}
                          
                              public static Singleton getInstance() {
                                  return INSTANCE;
                              }
                          }

2. Static block initialization:
                              public class Singleton {
                                  private static final Singleton INSTANCE;
                              
                                  static {
                                      INSTANCE = new Singleton();
                                  }
                              
                                  private Singleton() {}
                              
                                  public static Singleton getInstance() {
                                      return INSTANCE;
                                  }
                              }

3. Lazy initialization — not thread-safe:
                            public class Singleton {
                                private static Singleton instance;
                            
                                private Singleton() {}
                            
                                public static Singleton getInstance() {
                                    if (instance == null) {
                                        instance = new Singleton();
                                    }
                                    return instance;
                                }
                            }

4. Synchronized method
                      public class Singleton {
                          private static Singleton instance;
                      
                          private Singleton() {}
                      
                          public static synchronized Singleton getInstance() {
                              if (instance == null) {
                                  instance = new Singleton();
                              }
                              return instance;
                          }
                      }

5. Double-checked locking:
                        public class Singleton {
                            private static volatile Singleton instance;
                        
                            private Singleton() {}
                        
                            public static Singleton getInstance() {
                                if (instance == null) {
                                    synchronized (Singleton.class) {
                                        if (instance == null) {
                                            instance = new Singleton();
                                        }
                                    }
                                }
                                return instance;
                            }
                        }

Important: 
            volatile is essential here to ensure correct visibility and initialization behavior across threads.

6. Initialization-on-demand holder ⭐
                                    public class Singleton {
                                        private Singleton() {}
                                    
                                        private static class Holder {
                                            private static final Singleton INSTANCE = new Singleton();
                                        }
                                    
                                        public static Singleton getInstance() {
                                            return Holder.INSTANCE;
                                        }
                                    }

7. Enum Singleton:
                        public enum Singleton {
                            INSTANCE;
                        
                            public void execute() {
                                System.out.println("Executing...");
                            }
                        }


Usage:             Singleton.INSTANCE.execute();

8. Spring singleton bean:
                        import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
                        
                        @Service
                        public class PaymentService {
                            public void processPayment() {
                                System.out.println("Processing payment...");
                            }
                        }


By default, Spring creates one bean instance per bean definition per ApplicationContext. 
This is a container-managed singleton, not a JVM-wide singleton

============================================================================================================================================
EXPLAINATION AND LIMITATIONS:

1. LAZY INITIALIZATION:
                        This is a useful example of what not to use in a multithreaded application.
                        
                        Suppose two threads execute getInstance() simultaneously:
                                    1. Thread A checks instance == null.
                                    2. Thread B checks instance == null.
                                    3. Thread A creates an object.
                                    4. Thread B creates another object.
                        Both threads may return different instances.
                        Verdict: Not thread-safe
                        
                                    This implementation is unsafe even if it appears to work in local testing.
                                    A senior engineer must reason about possible interleavings, 
                                                not just typical execution.

2. Synchronized lazy initialization:

            The synchronized method acquires the class-level monitor.
            Only one thread at a time can execute the method.

            Benefit	                                    Cost
            Thread-safe lazy initialization	            Every call acquires the monitor
            Straightforward to understand	            Potential contention on a frequently called accessor
            Correct publication through synchronization	May be unnecessary overhead for a simple accessor
            
            This is correct, but we can avoid acquiring the monitor after the instance has been initialized.


3.Double-checked locking:

            Why are there two checks?
                        1. The outer check avoids synchronization on the normal path after initialization.
                        2. The inner check prevents a second thread from creating another instance after it acquires the lock.

            Why is volatile essential?
            
            Consider this line:
                        private static volatile AppConfig instance;


            In the Java Memory Model, 
                        volatile provides visibility and ordering guarantees 
                                    for reads and writes to the field.
                        It prevents unsafe publication caused by the problematic reordering 
                                    that double-checked locking without volatile could permit.
            
            Without volatile, a thread could observe a non-null reference without 
                        the required guarantees that the object's initialization is visible.

            Property:	                                    Result:
            Lazy initialization	                        Yes
            Thread-safe instance creation	            Yes
            Synchronization after initialization	Not on the fast path
            Complexity	                                    Higher than necessary for many applications
            Java 21 compatible	                        Yes

L6 insight: 
            Correctness comes first. 
            Double-checked locking is valid in modern Java when implemented correctly, 
            but it is not automatically the best implementation. 
            Prefer simpler initialization-on-demand or enum approaches when their semantics fit.

4.nitialization-on-demand holder idiom:

            This is an excellent default for a lazily initialized Singleton in plain Java when you need no extra lifecycle controls.
            Why does this work?
                        - Loading AppConfig does not by itself initialize Holder.
                        - The JVM initializes Holder when Holder.INSTANCE is first accessed.
                        - Class initialization is synchronized by the JVM, so competing threads cannot initialize the holder independently.
                        - The initialized instance is safely published.

            Property:	                                    Result:
            Lazy initialization	                        Yes
            Thread-safe	                                    Yes
            Explicit synchronized accessor	            Not needed
            volatile	                                    Not needed
            Readability	                                    Good
            Limitation	                                    Does not by itself solve serialization, reflection, or lifecycle concerns

For a conventional lazily initialized, plain-Java Singleton, this is often my preferred implementation.
