5. Singleton in Spring Boot — the most relevant production discussion

A critical concurrency question
            Suppose a Spring service is a singleton:
                                                @Service
                                                public class CustomerService {
                                                
                                                    private String currentCustomerId;
                                                
                                                    public void process(String customerId) {
                                                        currentCustomerId = customerId;
                                                        // Process customer
                                                    }
                                                }
            
            
            Two requests can invoke this service concurrently. 
            One request may overwrite currentCustomerId 
            while the other request is processing.
            
            The Singleton scope itself is not the problem. 
            Mutable request-specific instance state in a shared service is the problem.
            
            Prefer stateless services:
                                    @Service
                                    public class CustomerService {
                                    
                                        public void process(String customerId) {
                                            // Keep request-specific data local
                                            String currentCustomerId = customerId;
                                            // Process customer
                                        }
                                    }
                                    
            
            This is a strong interview point: 
                        Spring singleton beans are shared objects, 
                                    so their design must account for concurrent requests.


