Icon	Concern	            Problem	                                    Solution
🌐	Distributed systems	One instance per JVM, not per cluster	Distributed coordination if needed
🔄	Thread safety	Threads may modify shared data simultaneously	Statelessness, immutability, synchronization
🧪	Testing	            State may leak between tests	            Dependency injection, isolated tests
♻️	Lifecycle	            Resources need proper cleanup	            Lifecycle management
🚦	Resources	            Too many connections or tasks	            Pooling, bounded concurrency
🛡️	Availability	Instance disappears when its process stops	Recovery and redundancy
🧩	Extensibility	Tight coupling to a concrete class	            Interfaces and dependency injection

🔒 SINGLETON — WHAT CAN GO WRONG?
│
├── 🌐 1. Distributed Systems
│      ❌ One instance per JVM ≠ one instance across all servers.
│      ✅ Use distributed coordination only when required.
│
├── 🔄 2. Shared Mutable State
│      ❌ Multiple threads can modify the same data concurrently.
│      ✅ Prefer stateless services, immutable objects, or synchronization.
│
├── 🧪 3. Testing
│      ❌ Global state can leak from one test into another.
│      ✅ Use dependency injection and isolate test data.
│
├── ♻️ 4. Lifecycle
│      ❌ Creating an instance does not manage all its resources.
│      ✅ Manage initialization, cleanup, and resource ownership.
│
├── 🚦 5. Resource Exhaustion
│      ❌ One instance can still create too many connections or tasks.
│      ✅ Use connection pools, bounded concurrency, and backpressure.
│
├── 🛡️ 6. Availability
│      ❌ If the process stops, its Singleton instance disappears.
│      ✅ Design for restart, recovery, and redundancy.
│
└── 🧩 7. Extensibility
       ❌ Hard-coded Singleton access tightly couples the code.
       ✅ Use interfaces and dependency injection where appropriate.


============================================================================================================================================
mportant distributed-systems distinction
Imagine your Spring Boot application runs in four Kubernetes pods.
- Each pod has its own JVM.
- Each JVM can have its own Spring application context.
- A singleton-scoped bean normally exists separately in each context.
Therefore, you can have four instances of the same service across four pods.

If the actual requirement is "only one scheduler may execute a job across all pods," 
a Java Singleton will not satisfy it. You need an appropriate distributed coordination mechanism, 
such as a database-backed lease or a distributed lock, designed with failure handling and lease expiry in mind.

Do not introduce distributed locks merely because a service is singleton-scoped. 
First establish the business invariant that must hold across nodes.

