7. Google DeepMind L6 / Amazon Lead — how to explain your choice

A polished answer should cover the decision, the Java guarantee, and the production limitation.
Sample interview answer · approximately 60–90 seconds

            “Singleton is a creational design pattern that controls object creation so that one instance is shared within a defined scope.
            
            In plain Java, I would consider the initialization-on-demand holder idiom for lazy initialization because 
            JVM class initialization gives me thread-safe initialization without adding 
            synchronization to the accessor. If I need robust enum semantics, 
            including built-in serialization guarantees, I would consider an enum Singleton.
            
            In a Spring Boot application, I would normally rely on Spring's singleton 
            bean scope rather than introduce a separate static Singleton. 
            I would keep service beans stateless or otherwise thread-safe 
            because the same bean may handle concurrent requests.
            
            From a distributed-systems perspective, 
                        I would also make the scope explicit: 
                                    a JVM-local Singleton does not guarantee a single instance across multiple pods. 
            If the business requirement requires cross-node coordination, 
            I would choose a distributed coordination mechanism rather than relying on this pattern.
            
            Ultimately, I would choose the simplest implementation 
                        that satisfies the lifecycle, concurrency, and scope requirements.”



